Starting his reign with back-to-back victories against Malta in April helpe=
d secure a place in the playoffs for the 2027 World Cup, and McArdle=E2=80=
=99s team host Portugal on Friday at Inver Park in the first leg of their f=
irst-round playoff. He says his career experiences to date have aligned tow=
ards this =E2=80=9Cspecial moment in time=E2=80=9D with Northern Ireland.

The 46-year-old and his wife, Tammy, were both born, raised in and have nev=
er left the North Lanarkshire village of Moodiesburn near Glasgow. =E2=80=
=9CMy wife is now a social carer in the village, looking after the old budd=
ies that raised us =E2=80=93 we=E2=80=99ve always been from families that g=
ive for others and I think coaching aligns with that easily, because ultima=
tely that=E2=80=99s what coaching is,=E2=80=9D McArdle tells Moving the Goa=
lposts.

=E2=80=9CI=E2=80=99m a servant leader. I=E2=80=99m very comfortable that wa=
y, as an experienced coach, being vulnerable and open. That servant leaders=
hip style, I wanted to make sure that the [Northern Ireland] players are aw=
are of that right away.=E2=80=9D

Now a Uefa Pro licence holder, McArdle was influenced by watching videos of=
 the former Milan and Italy coach Arrigo Sacchi carrying out drills around =
a 4-4-2 formation. McArdle believes a more flexible approach is needed with=
 Northern Ireland, which is why his team deployed a variety of tactics acro=
ss his first four games in charge: April=E2=80=99s wins over Malta and June=
=E2=80=99s narrow 2-1 defeats by Switzerland and Turkey.


=E2=80=98To take that step up, sometimes you need to take a step backwards =
and take a few hits as well,=E2=80=99 says Northern Ireland=E2=80=99s Micha=
el McArdle. Photograph: Irish FA


=E2=80=9CI deliberately didn=E2=80=99t go with one strict formation or styl=
e. For a nation such as ourselves, from a population perspective, we=E2=80=
=99re the 150th nation in the world and we=E2=80=99re sitting 50th [in the =
Fifa women=E2=80=99s world rankings] because we do punch above our weight f=
or passion for the sport.

=E2=80=9CWe could easily just sit behind the ball and try to get through an=
d finish with the most points in League B and then just go to the playoffs =
and sit behind the ball again and just try and stay in the game. For me, th=
ough, and for us as a nation, we=E2=80=99re trying to go: =E2=80=98Let=E2=
=80=99s take a step beyond this, let=E2=80=99s sustainably now move towards=
 competing on a regular basis at a higher level.=E2=80=99

=E2=80=9CTo take that step, sometimes you need to take a step backwards and=
 take a few hits as well. I wanted us to be exposed and embrace [being unco=
mfortable]. I kept referencing =E2=80=98playoff ready=E2=80=99, because =E2=
=80=98playoff ready=E2=80=99 is where we=E2=80=99ll be tested at the highes=
t level. We learned so much about ourselves.

=E2=80=9CThe game of football is a living organism =E2=80=93 you can=E2=80=
=99t predict what happens. We need to be ready for everything as a small na=
tion. If we stick to one strict style of play, we won=E2=80=99t be adaptabl=
e when it comes to those moments.=E2=80=9D

McArdle, a former goalkeeper, is eager to develop many of Northern Ireland=
=E2=80=99s younger players, having done so in Scotland. He says he had oppo=
rtunities to leave the Scottish game before but the timing had not felt rig=
ht. This time, conversations with senior figures at the Irish FA such as An=
gela Platt, the director of women=E2=80=99s football, Aaron Hughes, technic=
al director, and Conrad Kirkwood, the now former president, made him realis=
e =E2=80=9Cat every meeting everything was just so aligned with my passion =
and my skill set=E2=80=9D. He also felt a bond between Glasgow and Belfast =
that =E2=80=9Cjust felt like family=E2=80=9D.


Northern Ireland=E2=80=99s Joely Andrews jumps for joy after scoring during=
 Euro 2025 qualifying at Windsor Park, Belfast. Photograph: Liam McBurney/P=
A


Northern Ireland reached Euro 2022 but have never played at a Women=E2=80=
=99s World Cup. McArdle is under no illusions about the size of the obstacl=
es standing between his team and next summer=E2=80=99s tournament in Brazil=
. Even if they can overcome Portugal =E2=80=93 a huge task in itself =E2=80=
=93 then either Croatia or Iceland would await in the second round of playo=
ffs in December. If Northern Ireland were to progress, they could still be =
the lowest-ranked of the eight European playoff winners.

If so =E2=80=93 because only the seven highest-ranked of the eight round-tw=
o playoff winners are guaranteed places in Brazil =E2=80=93 it would requir=
e Northern Ireland to contest a further round, in the inter-confederation p=
layoffs in February. That potential three-stage path is something akin to a=
 =E2=80=9Cmovie script=E2=80=9D, he says, but adds: =E2=80=9CThere are six =
probably history-making results to go and get.

=E2=80=9CGoing toe-to-toe with Portugal is a great opportunity for us to te=
st ourselves. When I first came into the post, I was very bullish to say =
=E2=80=98six points against Malta was a necessity=E2=80=99 and we had to pu=
t ourselves under that pressure =E2=80=93 now the opportunity to do somethi=
ng special, that=E2=80=99s the piece we=E2=80=99re all excited about.=E2=80=
=9D

