4ce" alt=3D"Jonathan Wilson" width=3D"110" height=3D"90"></td>
</tr></table></td></tr>
<tr><td style=3D"border-top:2px dashed;border-top-color:#999999;background-=
color:#FFFFFF;padding-bottom:8px">=C2=A0</td></tr>
<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The language of football is a fine and hig=
hly nuanced dialect. The majority of people involved in the game speak it w=
ith a high degree of sophistication. Minor deviations from the familiar, al=
most imperceptible to outsiders, can set off alarm bells: what=E2=80=99s re=
ally going on there? Why isn=E2=80=99t he happy? What=E2=80=99s she hinting=
 at? But it=E2=80=99s an argot developed to talk about football, and when a=
pplied to other subjects, it can sound extremely jarring.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Erling Haaland is one of the brighter, mor=
e engaging players. There=E2=80=99s an appealing quirkiness to him that sug=
gests he has a higher-than-usual level of self-awareness. He doesn=E2=80=99=
t mind stepping outside the norms, being a bit different. But consider his =
assessment of <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001=
.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEBgtjhYSJYcaNsDra0BxnPg2w_TPTsGe8=
dzC3LgZ2LyKnn-sP68IScLWUaLcMhBhtWh37j_0uH7moyMt3foTPXBi1ZlNvmUIdxbZ1ZmwFTyp=
ax51qJiaAPJp6-dhpAmlXzizFB75_94GoYkymFKWP504uW5UoXYy-Cg5KANtIMu89mHQnyO4YRF=
ViwihcafKCbJncwJ8gx6koLSxaZt1lPlJZRoQbvWaQbG9yCV2o7m9h-N-UHjlfBrpaTv7zYuLP4=
Htu_P-98bL6fgX5Y0mnHhGDo_69RCHuTafMWn8IgwIQRRLhVIUYAwf0HnOvPI4pk/4ut/P4AHzI=
JHTk61jwJ6Ubb34A/h8/h001.is7YxzOGltCpt2mV_ioKeYrf2z27iJSLMpiOlVavnuQ" style=
=3D"color: #0077B6;">Manchester City=E2=80=99s 3-0 win</a> at Liverpool on =
Sunday.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CIt was tough,=E2=80=9D he said. =
=E2=80=9CIt was hard but we showed amazing character from back to front. So=
 proud of everyone. [It=E2=80=99s] important to stay strong mentally, stick=
 together and do what you can do something about, and the thing we can do s=
omething about is to play good football and fight together. That=E2=80=99s =
the only thing we can do.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Strip away the context and he could be tal=
king about an injury crisis, or perhaps the resignation of a manager or a s=
uspension to a key player. The tropes are familiar: it was hard but we stuc=
k together and so we prevailed. =E2=80=9CYou want to win games, it=E2=80=99=
s as simple as that,=E2=80=9D he went on. =E2=80=9CPerform well, play good =
football and win games. This was a mental battle, [it=E2=80=99s] never easy=
 coming back from internationals.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">But of course this wasn=E2=80=99t really a=
bout coming back from internationals; that=E2=80=99s not what made this dif=
ficult for City. This was about the verdict of the independent panel lookin=
g into the charges brought against City, the fact that the club have been f=
ound guilty of the<a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/=
u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEBgtjhYSJYcaNsDra0BxnPgjpYPVo=
iFx3CnJVfcozyOyiCmN1lGPOgZk-r4dU9zNZGvaGEhrBIJxvwVtzL37MrDigzz795uEbG--YrrY=
T0UuH8oHC_pZoQad9Cg6c0oBWCrZxSDSJNzs1t2VQ9YqBx_9-vz_R_XGY51rTVKqVI8Dcn2KZR-=
HnUucK62iy4mE_J170oeN-D2gzYVSqmOXYorPDWt3fJRL6TipLqvoYC2X3kT_PYdNr_NSGkuA50=
ViXlD2LBNhvtGFnqHyWaJjVhCIYjfoZLNStQ5MHFSy8VdyGO-RYaMqJF2uuyuEwnOCUbIb05J1O=
N-ZUXNeqaOgH--DhqzzdCSQL6Vn50hOEhxMQ/4ut/P4AHzIJHTk61jwJ6Ubb34A/h9/h001.EPb=
9oWm_GyscUIElycMGwzIo05nS9kLWYR0hkpVeLzs" style=3D"color: #0077B6;"> greate=
st act of sustained cheating</a> in the history of English football. Nobody=
 knows what=E2=80=99s coming next.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The gravity of the situation still seems t=
o have not dawned on many in football. City, as is their right, continue to=
 maintain their innocence and have appealed. The propaganda battle goes on.=
 But this is no longer about whether the financial regulations were fair. I=
f City had objected to the regulations they had every opportunity to raise =
objections and vote against them. They did not. They agreed to the regulati=
ons, and while everybody else played to them, they did not.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The excuse that they only spent what other=
s did but that they operated under stricter controls because of a lack of h=
istoric success is not merely irrelevant but entirely bogus: in the nine ye=
ars under investigation, City=E2=80=99s net spending on transfers, before t=
aking into account wages, was over =C2=A31.2bn ($1.6m), around 50% more tha=
n any other Premier League side. And for all their talk of being proud disr=
uptors, of rebels taking on the =E2=80=9Cred cartel=E2=80=9D, that spending=
 transformed City into the most dominant side English football has ever kno=
wn, with six league titles in seven years. They are, precisely, an argument=
 for why spending controls are necessary.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">None of that, of course, is Haaland=E2=80=
=99s fault. He is a footballer offered a very competitive salary to play al=
ongside very good players for a very good manager for a club for which his =
father played. This is another of football=E2=80=99s absurdities: it=E2=80=
=99s always the manager and players who end up becoming the faces of the in=
stitution, being quizzed about things that have very little to do with them=
 as the executives and directors responsible lurk away from public scrutiny=
.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CMy message to the players since w=
e were together three days ago, when they all came back from their national=
 teams, has been quite clear,=E2=80=9D Enzo Maresca said. =E2=80=9CGuys, th=
e noise will always be there so it=E2=80=99s about how we learn to deal wit=
h the noise and it=E2=80=99s our duty to make our noise. Our noise is to wi=
n games in the way we did today and to be happy.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Which on the one hand is absurd: nobody kn=
ows whether the three points City claimed at Anfield will still be theirs b=
y Christmas. Nobody knows what division they will be playing in next season=
. Nobody knows how bad the sanction could be. And this is not some unfortun=
ate event that has befallen City; this is the direct consequence of the act=
ions of their directors. But on the other, what else can players and manage=
rs do? How else can they respond to this situation other than by being prof=
essional and trying to win games, even if none of it ultimately means anyth=
ing?</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">What this is emphatically not, though, is =
just another football crisis from which City can move on by winning footbal=
l matches. This is a crisis unlike anything English football has ever known=
, and it is one with which football=E2=80=99s language is struggling to cop=
e.</p>
