<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">England had performed well at the Euros in=
 2004, inspired by the 18-year-old Wayne Rooney, and there was a sense that=
 if only he hadn=E2=80=99t broken a metatarsal in the quarter-final against=
 Portugal, they might finally have ended what at the time was a 38-year wai=
t for a trophy. But their form had been less impressive after the tournamen=
t, with a 2-2 draw away to Austria exposing the defensive shortcomings of p=
airing Steven Gerrard and Frank Lampard in midfield. A 2-0 victory over Wal=
es set England=E2=80=99s World Cup qualification on track, though, just so =
long as they didn=E2=80=99t slip up away to Azerbaijan on 13 October 2004.<=
/p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The day before the game was bright and war=
m, but during Sven-G=C3=B6ran Eriksson=E2=80=99s press conference, a storm =
blew in, carrying with it sand from the deserts of Turkmenistan. The temper=
ature dropped dramatically. By the following morning, everything was cast i=
n a strange brown pall. The marquee where the post-match press conferences =
were supposed to be staged blew away. Conditions were dreadful, cold, with =
a driving rain. Journalists, protecting their laptops, wrote their reports =
from under their desks as a Michael Owen goal midway through the first half=
 gave England a 1-0 win.</p>
